{"name":"Cool Air of Debonair","hex":"#28394d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cool","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a mildly low temperature.","Allowing or suggesting heat relief."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A moderate or refreshing state of cold; moderate temperature of the air between hot and cold; coolness.","A calm temperament."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cool"},{"term":"air","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Airoran."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The substance constituting Earth's atmosphere: a gaseous mixture of nitrogen, oxygen, and various trace gases.","The substance of the atmosphere seen as an agency of freshness."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/air"},{"term":"debonair","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Gracious, courteous.","Suave, urbane and sophisticated."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Debonaire behaviour; graciousness."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/debonair"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cool Air","blocks":[{"text":"\"Cool Air\" is a short story by the American horror fiction writer H. P. Lovecraft, written in March 1926 and published in the March 1928 issue of Tales of Magic and Mystery."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Cool_Air.jpg/330px-Cool_Air.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cool_Air","wikidata":"Q2071759"},{"title":"Atmosphere of Earth","blocks":[{"text":"The atmosphere of Earth consists of a layer of mixed gas that is retained by gravity, surrounding the Earth's surface. It contains variable quantities of suspended aerosols and particulates that create weather features such as clouds and hazes. The atmosphere serves as a protective buffer between the Earth's surface and outer space. It shields the surface from most meteoroids and ultraviolet solar radiation, reduces diurnal temperature variation – the temperature extremes between day and night, and keeps it warm through heat retention via the greenhouse effect. The atmosphere redistributes heat and moisture among different regions via air currents, and provides the chemical and climate conditions that allow life to exist and evolve on Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg/330px-Top_of_Atmosphere.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atmosphere_of_Earth","wikidata":"Q3230"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/H._P._Lovecraft_in_Brooklyn%2C_11_July_1931.png/500px-H._P._Lovecraft_in_Brooklyn%2C_11_July_1931.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/H._P._Lovecraft_in_Brooklyn%2C_11_July_1931.png/500px-H._P._Lovecraft_in_Brooklyn%2C_11_July_1931.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"H. P. Lovecraft in Brooklyn, July 11, 1931","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AH._P._Lovecraft_in_Brooklyn%2C_11_July_1931.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Tales_of_Magic_and_Mystery_March_1928.jpg/500px-Tales_of_Magic_and_Mystery_March_1928.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Tales_of_Magic_and_Mystery_March_1928.jpg/1280px-Tales_of_Magic_and_Mystery_March_1928.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Tales_of_Magic_and_Mystery_March_1928.jpg/500px-Tales_of_Magic_and_Mystery_March_1928.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Issue of Tales of Magic and Mystery that Cool Air first appeared in, Ma